  ex:l4-ecdf-river-chart a cd:ChartDefinition ; cd:chartType cd:ObservationExplorer ; cd:observationView "ecdf" ; cd:compactObservations true ; skos:prefLabel "Exact threshold fractions for 50 samples"@en ; cd:body "Read horizontally from a threshold to its included cumulative fraction. Each single observation adds 1/50."@en ; cd:xAxisLabel "Nitrate threshold"@en ; cd:xAxisUnit "mg/L" ; cd:yAxisLabel "Fₙ(x)"@en ; cd:thresholdMinimum 0.0 ; cd:thresholdMaximum 26.0 ; cd:thresholdInitial 12.3 ; cd:thresholdStep 0.1 ; cd:hasObservationSeries ex:l4-ecdf-river-series ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-ecdf-shapes a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Different shapes, identical mean and SD"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: four synthetic detector series**\n\n100 observations each; all have mean **50**, sample SD **10 a.u.**\n\n**Interpretation**\n\n- **Uniform:** nearly steady rise across its range.\n- **Normal-like:** faster rise near the centre, slower in the tails.\n- **Right-skewed:** rapid early rise, then a long upper tail.\n- **Bimodal:** two rising regions separated by a plateau.\n\nSteep regions mean many observations in a short interval; a plateau means none in that interval. An ECDF is not a density curve."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-ecdf-crossing a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Crossing curves: which series is higher depends on x"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: compare response distributions**\n\nFocus on the uniform and normal-like series, each n = 100. Compare the curves below and above 50 a.u.\n\n**Interpretation**\n\nTheir curves cross: one can include a larger fraction at a low threshold and a smaller fraction at a higher threshold. ==No single mean or universal ranking answers every threshold question.==\n\nA higher ECDF at x means **more values ≤ x**, not larger measurement values. This descriptive comparison is not a significance test or proof that one analytical method is better."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-ecdf-shapes-chart a cd:ChartDefinition ; cd:chartType cd:ObservationExplorer ; cd:observationView "ecdf" ; cd:compactObservations true ; skos:prefLabel "Four shapes as cumulative fractions"@en ; cd:body "100 observations per series. Compare threshold counts; the four curves are not fitted models."@en ; cd:xAxisLabel "Response threshold"@en ; cd:xAxisUnit "a.u." ; cd:yAxisLabel "Fₙ(x)"@en ; cd:thresholdMinimum 20.0 ; cd:thresholdMaximum 100.0 ; cd:thresholdInitial 50.0 ; cd:thresholdStep 0.1 ; cd:hasObservationSeries ex:l4-shapes-uniform-series, ex:l4-shapes-normal-series, ex:l4-shapes-skewed-series, ex:l4-shapes-bimodal-series ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-ecdf-inverse a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Read backwards: inverse ECDF versus linear interpolation"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: the same 50 river samples**\n\nAsk for the smallest observed x with Fₙ(x) ≥ **.25**. It is observation ceil(50 × .25) = **rank 13**, hence **6.5 mg/L**.\n\n**Interpretation**\n\nThe generalized inverse selects an observed value. Linear interpolation instead uses h = 1 + 49 × .25 = **13.25**, between 6.5 and 7: **6.625 mg/L**.\n\n==Both conventions are explicit, but they are not identical.== At p = 0, software conventions need separate care; the generalized inverse here is defined for 0 < p ≤ 1."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .

}
